    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. In fact, when I was brought up in a family that believed in hospitality and friendliness and good nature attitudes. When I was a young sales rep for the Phillips Company, I drove around five states and sold our products. I remember my great grandfather telling me that when you sell something, you don't use your mouth, you use your ears. You listen. And I remember those lessons. And frankly, a lot of the stories and a lot of the people I met in my early 20s driving around outstate Minnesota and rural North Dakota and Nebraska. our stories and remember to this day, and very much the same template I employed in the campaign, which was listening to people. If you're going to be a member of Congress, it serves you well to like people and like hearing their stories. And I was also taught that if two people, I always agree, you only need one of them. And I say that tongue in cheek, but it's true. I learn more from people who have different life experiences, have different perspectives, and see things differently. And that is my responsibility. And it's actually what makes campaigning fun. and particularly serving in Congress remarkable. So you really enjoy the campaign. I love it. It's fun. But like anything else in life, you've got to make something compelling",
