  {
    "episode_id": 90843,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90843.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "So what people come to me to talk about regularly is not dissimilar from what they were talking about during the campaign, which is starting with health care and the cost of health care. The fact that we have so many people who are one illness away from bankruptcy and who are imploring that we figure out a way to cover more people and reduce the cost of care. Young people are coming to me both here and in Washington in big groups advocating for climate change efforts, gun violence prevention legislation. By the way, which we are addressing. I don't want to diverge too much, but we've passed 435 bills in the House already since January 3rd, only 17 of which have actually received a vote in the Senate. And I say that because we are doing more than some might suspect again based on watching cable news. Climate change, gun violence, prevention, health care. There's no question that in the third district in Minnesota, because for reasons you're well aware, anti-Semitism has been on the minds of certain constituents. Veterans Affairs, in fact, just last night, held a community conversation. about veterans affairs, had a wonderful turnout, about 16 people spoke. Opioid addiction is on the radar screen, had a conversation about that. And what I ran on, Lonnie, was reform, relative to campaign finance, relative to ethics reform and voter protection and electoral security. Because there are some clear and present dangers that Bob Mueller, just the other day in his press conference, referred to And it is a responsibility of ours in Congress and certainly the President to address these dangerous truths that exist right now, including election security.",
