  {
    "episode_id": 90843,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90843.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "So you had mentioned Bob Mueller in terms of election security, and obviously, HR1, the first bill out of the shoot was about election security and ethics and reform, that kind of things that you spoke to throughout the campaign trial. But obviously, with the conclusion of Reverend Mueller's investigation and his statements recently, how has what he said impacted how you think the House should proceed in terms of holding the president accountable?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Bob Mueller was mentioned by the interlocutor in relation to election security.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Reverend Mueller concluded his investigation and made recent public statements.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "HR1 was the first bill introduced concerning election security, ethics, and reform.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The topics discussed during the campaign trail included election security, ethics, and reform.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The conclusions of Mueller's investigation and his recent statements have influenced how the speaker views the appropriate course for the House regarding presidential accountability.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Mueller's findings and statements carry significant weight in shaping legislative responses to presidential conduct.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The House has a responsibility to act on evidence related to presidential misconduct.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Publicly available reports from investigations like Mueller’s constitute valid and trustworthy evidence for policy decisions.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker holds a normative view that accountability for the president is both necessary and achievable through institutional means.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The audience expects or values reasoned discussion based on investigative outcomes when considering political accountability.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90843,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90843.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, the report, for those who have read the report while it doesn't call for impeachment or prosecution, if you will, I think Mr. Mueller made it clear why it didn't in his press conference the other day. He laid out, in my estimation, some very compelling facts that we cannot dismiss. And in my estimation, What has changed are two things. One of which is that he was so clear in his recent press conference that what the country should be focused on more than anything is meddling in our elections. It happened in 2016. It's going to happen again in 2020 and as he said, every American should be mindful of it. What he didn't answer was how we should proceed, and that is because that's how the Constitution prescribes it. We are afforded a mechanism to remove a president if we feel necessary. We're not compelled to do so. And in my estimation, we have more to learn. And the report that we have read is redacted. I call attention to the 12 of the 14 cases referred to in the report that were sent to other agencies because they were outside the jurisdiction or the purview of this specific investigation. But those have all been redacted. We don't know what they are yet. So on the subject of impeachment and how to proceed, in my estimation, like most Democrats still, I believe we should continue down the legal paths that we have available to us. documents produced that we can read and better understand, solicit testimony from people who have not yet appeared. And in my estimation, most importantly, identify which of those 12 cases that were referred out of the Mueller investigation, if any of those have evidence of criminal activity. And if so, we have a responsibility to act because we have an oath to our Constitution, not to a party.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
