  {
    "episode_id": 90843,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90843.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "It's broadband. It's water and sewer and it is comprehensive. We are also a nation that is in some ways a teenager amongst adults. We are a nation that is a little over two and a half centuries old and compared to some of our peers that have literally generations and generations of infrastructure underneath them. And that's hard to come to grips with. But the other epiphany is that Democrats and Republicans all agree and recognize what we have to do. There's not a lot of debate about what we need to do. The debate is how you pay for it. And we find the resources to go to war. We find hundreds of billions of dollars a year to defend our country. And we should be able to deploy adequate resources to ensure the foundation of the country is secure, and there are ways to do so, and we're working on them right now. And I would argue personally that I'm a proponent of carbon fee and dividend, which is putting a price on carbon, returning the proceeds to taxpayers. It provides an incentive to start migrating to clean energy and a disincentive to continue carbon-based fuels. I'm a proponent of potentially enhancing that so that we create a national infrastructure bank of resources derived from pricing carbon. And it is more broad-based than just a gas tax. and it's comprehensive, and it also will allow us to actually address climate change and our infrastructure needs. So I'm advocating for that. I've shared that with the White House and with others in Congress, and we'll see where it goes.",
